Keep a dlopen reference to libvulkan on every init_loader path — PUNKTFUNK LOCAL PATCH.

Not upstream. `Context::init_loader` only dlopens the loader when it must resolve
`vkGetInstanceProcAddr` itself (`addr == nullptr`). When the first call arrives with a
caller-owned `addr` — `pyrowave_create_device` passes the embedder's — no module
reference is taken at all, yet `volkInitializeCustom` binds every global trampoline
(vkEnumerateInstanceVersion and friends) through that address, and the trampolines
live in libvulkan.so.1.

The embedder's handle is not ours to keep alive: ash::Entry owns a
libloading::Library, and when the last Entry drops the loader's refcount hits zero
and dlclose unmaps libvulkan. `loader_init_once` still reads true, so the next
`init_loader(nullptr)` early-returns and `volkGetInstanceVersion` jumps into
unmapped memory. Reproduces deterministically on Linux: create + destroy a
handle-based device, then call `pyrowave_create_default_device` — SIGSEGV inside
volkGetInstanceVersion.

WHAT IT DOES. Hoists the POSIX module dlopen out of the `!addr` branch so a
reference is held on every path, exactly like the Windows branch's static
LoadLibrary HMODULE that is never freed. The held module honors the same
GRANITE_VULKAN_LIBRARY override and soname fallback order as upstream. When `addr`
is supplied the dlopen is best-effort: a caller whose procs live outside
libvulkan.so.1 can steer the hold via GRANITE_VULKAN_LIBRARY. libvulkan is a
process-global loader — a permanent reference is the intended lifetime.

